  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Severe thunderstorms and some instances of flash\nflooding are possible on Sunday.\n\nSome residual convective activity may be ongoing on Sunday \nmorning. This remains to be seen and will be guided by the\ndegree of overnight instability and boundary placement. How the\natmosphere recovers through the day should ultimately dictate\nthe degree of severe thunderstorm threat during the core diurnal\nheating hours. The ingredients are certainly present for a more\nimpactful event if meteorological parameters all come together.\n\nLooking a bit more closely at this event, a broad mid/upper\nridge persist across the Four Corners eastward to the\nsouthwestern Atlantic. Amplified flow north of this expansive\nanticyclone will continue to be a conduit to active convection,\nmany in the form of mesoscale convective systems (MCS). As\nmentioned above, a remnant MCS might still be ongoing early \nSunday across the region. While above average heights persist, \nexpect some non-zero height falls to cross the area in response \nto an approaching shortwave. Within this northwesterly flow \naloft, vertical shear is ample for the time of year, generally \naround 30 to 35 knots. Depending on model choice, mixed-layer \nCAPE values approach 1,500 to 2,500 J/kg. The combination of \nthese two will easily support a risk of severe weather. While \ndamaging wind gusts are the most likely threat, other severe \ntypes loom if storms can remain discrete in nature.\n\nAdditionally, a Slight Risk for excessive rainfall continues\nalong much of the Alleghenies eastward along/north of I-66/U.S.\n50. Precipitable water values rise markedly, possibly into the 2\nto 2.25 inch range. While a single thunderstorm will be capable\nof torrential downpours, the bigger threat would be for repeat\nand/or training convection. The latter potential exists which\nsupports the flash flood risk. Although drought continues over\nmuch of the region, many areas have seen a one category\nimprovement over the past week. Convection will likely be\nongoing into Sunday night with the cold front not passing\nthrough until early Monday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Seasonably warm conditions and daily shower and\nthunderstorm chances are expected through Monday, before another\nround of storms are possible on Thursday.\n\nHigh pressure building across southern Quebec down into New\nEngland will favor a mostly quiet day for the area on Saturday.\nAn onshore component to the winds should favor a mix of clouds\nand sun through the day. Highs will rise into the mid/upper 80s\nwith any shower/thunderstorm chances confined to the Potomac\nHighlands across the central Shenandoah Valley toward I-64.\nRecent high-resolution models are showing an MCS racing across \nthe Ohio Valley late Saturday. Some of this could leak across \nthe Alleghenies, but confidence is low given a fairly mixed \nmodel signal.\n\nAs Sunday's cold front tracks offshore, upper-level zonal flow \nbringing drier air from the Great Lakes persists over the Mid-\nAtlantic through midweek, creating a break in the rain and \nthunderstorms. While temperatures & dewpoints drop a few degrees by \nTuesday in the wake of the front, temperatures should gradually \nincrease again in the latter half of the week as high pressure \nbuilds overhead. \n\nMeanwhile, an upper-level trough will begin to form over the \nNorthern Plains and then track across the Great Lakes late in the \nweek, with a strong cold front potentially moving through the Mid-\nAtlantic between Thursday and Friday. Medium-range algorithms are \nalready highlighting a potential for severe thunderstorms with this \nsystem. However, severe probabilities currently vary widely across \nmodels, with CSU ML probabilities highlighting only a marginal 5%-\n15% probability as of the 12Z model runs while the WxNext2 Mean AI \noutlook is already showing a 30%-45% probability. Deterministically, \nthe GFS/ECMWF also have not quite aligned on the timing and \nplacement of the trough 7 days out. Should this setup\nmaterialize, storms capable of heavy downpours and damaging \nwinds would be possible. As drought conditions continue to \nimprove area-wide, localized flooding becomes more of a concern \nwith any thunderstorms that form in the region. For now, this \nwill be a feature to continue to monitor as the synoptic setup \nbecomes more defined.",
